Fix the lazy-RNS 64-bit FMA kernel on narrow SIMD targets.

BatchFusedMulAddMontgomeryRepHwy's Uint64 specialization regroups the
MulEven/MulOdd products with Per4LaneBlockShuffle<3, 1, 2, 0>, which is only
correct when a vector holds at least four 64-bit lanes. On aarch64 NEON (and
on SSE2/SSSE3) a vector holds two 64-bit lanes, the shuffle degenerates, and
the accumulator receives a mix of low/high halves from different coefficients.
Every ApplyToWithRandomPad rotation (used by the LinPIR BSGS prepare phase)
then produces garbage.

Fall back to the portable scalar implementation when fewer than four 64-bit
lanes are available. AVX2/AVX-512 and other wide targets are unaffected.

--- a/shell_encryption/rns/rns_polynomial_hwy.cc
+++ b/shell_encryption/rns/rns_polynomial_hwy.cc
@@ -110,6 +110,15 @@
   const int N = hn::Lanes(d);
   const int64_t num_coeffs = a.size();
 
+  // The vectorized code below relies on a four-lane block shuffle to regroup
+  // the 64x64->128-bit products, so it is only valid when a vector holds at
+  // least four 64-bit lanes. Narrow SIMD targets such as NEON (two lanes)
+  // and SSE2/SSSE3 (two lanes) must use the scalar implementation instead.
+  if (N < 4) {
+    BatchFusedMulAddMontgomeryRepNoHwy(a, b, output);
+    return;
+  }
+
   // Generate the masks on the even lanes, which correspond to the lower 64 bits
   // of BigInt64 (unsigned 128-bit int) values in the output vector.
   uint8_t* mask_lo_bits = new uint8_t[(N + 7) / 8];
